Iterating Backwards in C::title
<i>[David Priver], October 24th, 2026</i>

::raw
  <article>

This is shorter than some of my other posts, but I keep seeing people
doing this wrong. It's even worse now in the LLM era.

Let's say you have a buffer and a counter and you need to iterate it
backwards for some reason:
::pre.C
  _Bool find_last(size_t count, int *haystack, 
                  int needle, size_t *where);

Almost everyone can do the forward version on autopilot:
::pre.C
  for(size_t i = 0; i < count; i++){
    if(haystack[i] == needle){
      *where = i;
      return 1;
    }
  }
  return 0;

So they try to do the same thing, just iterating backwards:

::pre.C
  for(size_t i = count - 1; i >= 0; i--){
    // find needle
  }
But that doesn't work at all! <tt>i >=0</tt> is tautologically true
(modern compilers will warn). Even worse, if count is 0, <tt>i</tt> immediately becomes
<tt>SIZE_MAX</tt>.

Ok, so they try the next version:

::pre.C
  for(ssize_t i = count - 1; i >= 0; i--){
    // find needle
  }
This works in practice with a signed index but introduces
some problems:
- Now your index variable is signed, which can cause problems when used in expressions due to
  C's conversion rules.
- <tt>ssize_t</tt> is a POSIX thing so you'd have to define it yourself on windows.
- If your type is 32 bit, you run into promotion problems and might even run into truncation problems.
- This spelling of the loop is overcomplicated and requires special inspection
  each time. Did you get the second-clause condition right?

Avoiding these problems brings us to the actual, proper way to iterate backwards in C:

::pre.C
  for(size_t i = count; i--; ){
    // find needle
  }

So much simpler! Let's break it down:
The key is the postdecrement operator on <tt>i</tt>. 
It decrements <tt>i</tt> but yields the value that <tt>i</tt> was before the operation.
In this case, this means that in the body of the loop <tt>i</tt> is always in bounds
as its value will be decremented after initialization so it is at most <tt>count - 1</tt>, 
while the condition checks that if it was previously 0 then the loop ends. <tt>i</tt> will
wrap around, but unsigned wrap-around is well defined in C so that is not a problem.

The final version:
::pre.C
  _Bool find_last(size_t count, int *haystack, 
                  int needle, size_t *where){
    for(size_t i = count; i--; ){
      if(haystack[i] == needle){
        *where = i;
        return 1;
      }
    }
    return 0;
  }

Doing it this way means you don't have to mess around with converting to a signed
loop index and look at how simple that loop is! You can tell at a glance
if someone has implemented the reverse loop properly or not.

So please! If you see someone trying to iterate backwards, show them this way
to do it!

::raw
  </article>
